Below is a condensed but fully-functional sketch of a minimal client library.  A production library would separate classes into files and add extensive tests and documentation, but the core ideas are illustrated.

```java
package io.example.k8s.logs;

import java.io.*;
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.*;
import java.security.KeyStore;
import java.security.cert.CertificateFactory;
import java.security.cert.X509Certificate;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Objects;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

/** Top-level facade */
public class LogClient {
    private final HttpClient client;
    private final String apiServer;
    private final String bearerToken;

    private LogClient(String apiServer, HttpClient client, String bearerToken) {
        this.apiServer  = apiServer.endsWith("/") ? apiServer.substring(0, apiServer.length() - 1) : apiServer;
        this.client     = client;
        this.bearerToken = bearerToken;
    }

    /* ---------- Builder for convenient configuration ---------- */
    public static class Builder {
        private String apiServer = "https://kubernetes.default.svc";     // in-cluster default
        private Duration connectTimeout = Duration.ofSeconds(10);
        private String bearerToken = readServiceAccountToken();
        private boolean trustClusterCA = true;
        private String caFile = "/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt";

        public Builder apiServer(String apiServer) { this.apiServer = apiServer; return this; }
        public Builder bearerToken(String token) { this.bearerToken = token; return this; }
        public Builder trustClusterCA(boolean trust, String caFile) { this.trustClusterCA = trust; this.caFile = caFile; return this; }

        public LogClient build() {
            HttpClient.Builder hc = HttpClient.newBuilder()
                    .connectTimeout(connectTimeout)
                    .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2);
            if (trustClusterCA) {
                hc.sslContext(Util.sslContextFromCA(caFile));
            }
            return new LogClient(apiServer, hc.build(), bearerToken);
        }

        private static String readServiceAccountToken() {
            File f = new File("/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token");
            if (f.exists()) try { return new String(java.nio.file.Files.readAllBytes(f.toPath())); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
            return null; // caller must set explicitly when running outside cluster
        }
    }

    /* ------------- Public API ------------- */
    public CompletableFuture<Void> streamLogs(LogRequest req, LogHandler handler) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(req);
        Objects.requireNonNull(handler);

        String path = String.format("%s/api/v1/namespaces/%s/pods/%s/log?container=%s&follow=%b&sinceSeconds=%d&timestamps=%b&tailLines=%d",
                apiServer,
                req.namespace(),
                req.podName(),
                req.container(),
                req.follow(),
                req.sinceSeconds(),
                req.timestamps(),
                req.tailLines());

        HttpRequest.Builder rb = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                .uri(URI.create(path))
                .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + bearerToken)
                .timeout(Duration.ofMinutes(30))
                .GET();

        // we expect an open-ended stream so we use BodyHandlers.ofLines()
        return client.sendAsync(rb.build(), HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofLines())
                .thenAccept(resp -> {
                    if (resp.statusCode() != 200) {
                        handler.onError(new IllegalStateException("HTTP " + resp.statusCode() + " – " + resp.body()));
                        return;
                    }
                    resp.body().forEach(handler::onLine);
                    handler.onClose();
                });
    }

    /* ------------- Helper records & interfaces ------------- */
    public record LogRequest(String namespace, String podName, String container, boolean follow, long sinceSeconds, boolean timestamps, int tailLines) {
        public static LogRequest follow(String ns, String pod, String container) {
            return new LogRequest(ns, pod, container, true, 0, false, 10);
        }
    }

    public interface LogHandler {
        void onLine(String line);
        default void onError(Throwable t) { t.printStackTrace(); }
        default void onClose() {}
    }

    /* ------------- Utility for TLS ------------- */
    private static class Util {
        static javax.net.ssl.SSLContext sslContextFromCA(String caPath) {
            try (InputStream in = new FileInputStream(caPath)) {
                CertificateFactory cf = CertificateFactory.getInstance("X.509");
                X509Certificate ca = (X509Certificate) cf.generateCertificate(in);
                KeyStore ks = KeyStore.getInstance(KeyStore.getDefaultType());
                ks.load(null, null);
                ks.setCertificateEntry("ca", ca);

                javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory tmf = javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory.getInstance(javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm());
                tmf.init(ks);

                javax.net.ssl.SSLContext sslContext = javax.net.ssl.SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
                sslContext.init(null, tmf.getTrustManagers(), null);
                return sslContext;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Unable to set up SSL context", e);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Key Points Explained
1. **Endpoint** – Uses GET /api/v1/namespaces/{ns}/pods/{pod}/log with query parameters follow, sinceSeconds, tailLines, timestamps, container as documented in Kubernetes 1.28.
2. **Streaming** – BodyHandlers.ofLines() turns the chunked transfer stream into a Stream<String>; back-pressure is managed by forEach consumer.
3. **TLS & Auth** – In-cluster default uses service-account token & CA certificate; outside cluster you pass token/kubeconfig in the builder.
4. **Library Design** –  Fluent builder + immutable LogRequest record; separation of concerns between request construction, HTTP layer, and user callback.
5. **Error Handling** – Non-200 responses routed to onError; library caller decides what to do (retry, escalate, etc.).
6. **Threading** – sendAsync relies on the client’s executor; caller may supply a custom executor for high-volume streaming.
7. **Extensibility** – Library could later expose additional options (limitBytes, pretty=true, etc.) or switch to WebSocket for SPDY replacement.
8. **RBAC** – Requires verb get on resource pods/log or pods in the given namespace.

Example Usage
