excellent_response:
Overview
This script turns a standard <form> that is split into several "pages" (each element has the class "step") into a wizard-style interface with Next/Previous navigation. When the user finally submits, the code collects every input value, logs the data, and clears the form so it can be filled again.

1. Collect DOM references
• steps – Array.from(document.querySelectorAll("form .step"))
  – querySelectorAll returns a NodeList; Array.from converts it to a true Array so we can later call indexOf().
• nextBtn – NodeList containing every element with the class .next-btn inside the form.
• prevBtn – NodeList containing every element with the class .previous-btn inside the form.
• form – The first <form> element found in the document.

2. Attach click listeners to navigation buttons
nextBtn.forEach(btn => btn.addEventListener("click", () => changeStep("next")));
prevBtn.forEach(btn => btn.addEventListener("click", () => changeStep("prev")));
Using the same changeStep() function for both directions avoids repetition; we simply pass which way we want to move.

3. Handle form submission
form.addEventListener("submit", e => {
  e.preventDefault(); // Keep the page from reloading
  const inputs = [];
  form.querySelectorAll("input").forEach(input => {
     const { name, value } = input; // ES6 destructuring
     inputs.push({ name, value });
  });
  console.log(inputs); // e.g., [ { name: "email", value: "foo@bar.com" } ]
  form.reset();        // Clears all fields so the form looks fresh
});
All <input> elements are gathered, even those on steps that are currently hidden, because they are still in the DOM.

4. changeStep(direction)
function changeStep(direction) {
  const active = document.querySelector(".active");
  let index = steps.indexOf(active); // Works because steps is a real Array
  active.classList.remove("active");
  if (direction === "next") index++; else index--;
  // NOTE: No boundary checks; going past ends would cause an error
  steps[index].classList.add("active");
}
The .active class is expected to control visibility via CSS (only the active step is displayed).

Edge cases & improvements
• There must be exactly one .active element when the page loads.
• Buttons are not disabled at the ends, so clicking Next on the last step will throw an error. You could add guards like `if (index < steps.length - 1)`.
• In production you would probably validate inputs before moving to the next step or before submitting, and replace console.log with an API call.

