Why the warning appears
-----------------------
Static-analysis tools such as IntelliJ IDEA, SpotBugs, or Error-Prone report “Non-atomic operation on volatile field 'modCount'” because `volatile` guarantees *visibility* only.  The expression `modCount++` is a three-step read-modify-write; two threads can interleave these steps and lose an update.

Three possible ways to silence the warning
-----------------------------------------
1. **Single-threaded use case** → simply drop the `volatile` keyword.
2. **Lock-based fix** → keep an int but put every access to `modCount` inside a `synchronized(this)` block.
3. **Lock-free fix (chosen below)** → replace the field with `AtomicInteger` and use its atomic methods.

Full patch (Java 8+)
--------------------
```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;
import java.util.ConcurrentModificationException;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class CircularArrayDeQueue<E> implements DeQueue<E> {
    private E[] array;
    private int front, rear, size;
    private final AtomicInteger modCount = new AtomicInteger(); // was: volatile int modCount
    private static final int DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 16;

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public CircularArrayDeQueue() {
        array = (E[]) new Object[DEFAULT_CAPACITY];
        front = rear = size = 0; // AtomicInteger starts at 0 automatically
    }

    public void pushFirst(E elem) {
        expandIfNeeded();
        front = (front - 1 + array.length) % array.length;
        array[front] = elem;
        size++;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();
    }

    public void pushLast(E elem) {
        expandIfNeeded();
        array[rear] = elem;
        rear = (rear + 1) % array.length;
        size++;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();
    }

    public E popFirst() {
        if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        E elem = array[front];
        array[front] = null;
        front = (front + 1) % array.length;
        size--;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();
        shrinkIfNeeded();
        return elem;
    }

    public E popLast() {
        if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        rear = (rear - 1 + array.length) % array.length;
        E elem = array[rear];
        array[rear] = null;
        size--;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();
        shrinkIfNeeded();
        return elem;
    }

    public void clear() {
        Arrays.fill(array, null);
        front = rear = size = 0;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();
    }

    private void resize(int newCap) {
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        E[] newArr = (E[]) new Object[newCap];
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            newArr[i] = array[(front + i) % array.length];
        }
        array = newArr;
        front = 0;
        rear = size;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();
    }

    /* ... expandIfNeeded, shrinkIfNeeded, isEmpty, etc. unchanged ... */

    private class DeQueueIterator implements Iterator<E> {
        private int current;
        private final int step;
        private final int expectedModCount;
        DeQueueIterator(int start, int step) {
            this.current = start;
            this.step = step;
            this.expectedModCount = modCount.get();
        }
        public boolean hasNext() {
            return current != rear;
        }
        public E next() {
            if (modCount.get() != expectedModCount) {
                throw new ConcurrentModificationException();
            }
            E item = array[current];
            current = (current + step + array.length) % array.length;
            return item;
        }
    }
}
```

Why this fixes the warning
--------------------------
* `AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet()` is a single compare-and-swap, therefore atomic.
* Reads via `get()` see a consistent value, satisfying the iterator’s fail-fast logic.
* `AtomicInteger` already supplies the same *happens-before* visibility guarantee that `volatile` provided, so the extra keyword is unnecessary.

Performance / safety note
-------------------------
Only `modCount` is now safe for concurrent access.  The deque’s structural operations are still *not* thread-safe; use `java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentLinkedDeque` if you need a fully concurrent container.

