Short Answer
-------------
You cannot "make sure that 0x0010 0000 is mapped" from a normal loadable kernel module, because that address is inside the *user* portion of the virtual address space.  On every recent x86 Linux kernel, kernel code and data live in the *high* portion of the address space (0xC0 0000 00 on 32-bit, 0xFFFF 8000 0000 0000 on 64-bit).  Instead, ask the kernel for executable memory and use the address it gives back.

Why 0x0010 0000 is unmapped
---------------------------
• x86-32 (no PAE, default config): user 0x0000 0000 – 0xBFFF FFFF, kernel 0xC000 0000 – 0xFFFF FFFF.  0x0010 0000 sits firmly in user space, therefore page tables for that address do *not* exist in kernel context.
• x86-64: user 0x0000 0000 0000 0000 – 0x0000 7FFF FFFF FFFF; kernel 0xFFFF 8000 0000 0000 upward.  Again, 0x0010 0000 is user.
Only a handful of early-boot identity-mapped pages and the fix-map live below PAGE_OFFSET, and a loadable module cannot modify those safely.

Correct way: allocate executable kernel memory
---------------------------------------------
1. Ask the kernel for pages:

   ```c
   #include <linux/module.h>
   #include <linux/vmalloc.h>
   #include <linux/mm.h>
   #include <linux/set_memory.h>

   static void *buf;
   static const unsigned char shellcode[] =
       "\x48\x31\xc0\x48\xff\xc0\xc3"; /* xor rax,rax; inc rax; ret */

   static int __init demo_init(void)
   {
       size_t sz = PAGE_ALIGN(sizeof(shellcode));

       /* 1. allocate writable, non-exec pages */
       buf = vmalloc(sz);
       if (!buf)
           return -ENOMEM;

       /* 2. copy code while pages are RW */
       memcpy(buf, shellcode, sizeof(shellcode));

       /* 3. convert pages to executable, read-only */
       set_memory_x((unsigned long)buf, sz >> PAGE_SHIFT);

       pr_info("shellcode mapped at %p\n", buf);

       /* 4. call it */
       ((void (*)(void))buf)();
       return 0;
   }

   static void __exit demo_exit(void)
   {
       size_t sz = PAGE_ALIGN(sizeof(shellcode));
       set_memory_nx((unsigned long)buf, sz >> PAGE_SHIFT); /* restore NX */
       vfree(buf);
   }
   module_init(demo_init);
   module_exit(demo_exit);
   MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
   ```

   On kernels built with CONFIG_STRICT_MODULE_RWX, `vmalloc` gives writable *and* non-executable memory; `set_memory_x()` flips the PTEs to executable after we finish writing, preserving W^X.

2. Older kernels (< 4.11) that lack `set_memory_x()` can use `module_alloc()` which returns pages already mapped with `PAGE_KERNEL_EXEC`.

Why **not** to force-map 0x0010 0000
------------------------------------
• You’d have to create your own page directory entries with `kernel_set_pte()` or by poking CR3.  This breaks KPTI and crashes the system on the first user access.
• That region might already be used by a user process; stealing it would violate process isolation.
• SMEP/SMAP will make the CPU fault if kernel code jumps into low addresses anyway.

If you *really* need a fixed virtual address (e.g., for a special device), you can use the per-architecture `fix_to_virt()` or `set_fixmap()` helpers, but they only work inside the predefined **fix-map** region (x86-64: 0xffff f580 0000 0000 and up), not at 0x0010 0000.

Bottom line: allocate memory the supported way, mark it executable with `set_memory_x()`, and forget about address 0x100000.

Below is a concise yet thorough walkthrough for “baby’s-first grep” in Rust. Copy–paste segments as you follow along.

──────────────── 1. Create the project ────────────────
$ cargo new rgrep    # short for “Rust grep”
$ cd rgrep

Cargo scaffolds:
  rgrep/
    Cargo.toml   # metadata & deps
    src/main.rs  # entry point

──────────────── 2. Parse CLI args ────────────────
Open src/main.rs and replace the stub with:

```rust
use std::{env, fs::File, io::{self, BufRead, BufReader}};
use std::process;

fn main() {
    // Expect pattern and an optional path; default to STDIN
    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();
    if args.len() < 2 || args.len() > 3 {
        eprintln!("Usage: rgrep <pattern> [file]");
        process::exit(1);
    }
    let pattern = &args[1];

    // Choose input source
    let reader: Box<dyn BufRead> = if args.len() == 3 {
        match File::open(&args[2]) {
            Ok(file) => Box::new(BufReader::new(file)),
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("Could not open {}: {}", &args[2], e);
                process::exit(1);
            }
        }
    } else {
        Box::new(BufReader::new(io::stdin()))
    };

    // Iterate lines and print matches
    for (idx, line) in reader.lines().enumerate() {
        let line = match line {
            Ok(l) => l,
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("Read error: {}", e);
                continue; // skip bad line, keep going
            }
        };
        if line.contains(pattern) {
            println!("{:>6}: {}", idx + 1, line);
        }
    }
}
```

Key points:
• &args[1] gives an &String which coerces to &str for contains().
• Box<dyn BufRead> abstracts over File vs STDIN without generics gymnastics.
• Error handling uses Result and early exits so the compiler is happy about exhaustive cases.

──────────────── 3. Build & run ────────────────
$ cargo run -- "fn main" src/main.rs
     Compiling rgrep v0.1.0 (…)
     Finished dev [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 1.2s
     Running `target/debug/rgrep "fn main" src/main.rs`
     12: fn main() {

STDIN example:
$ cat Cargo.toml | cargo run -- "serde"
      8: serde = "1"

──────────────── 4. Unit test the matcher ────────────────
Add below main():

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*; // bring main scope

    #[test]
    fn finds_substring() {
        let hay = "hello rustaceans";
        assert!(hay.contains("rust"));
    }
}
```

Run: $ cargo test

──────────────── 5. Next steps (optional) ────────────────
• Switch argument parsing to the “clap” crate for “rgrep –ignore-case –help”.
• Support Regex via the “regex” crate.
• Use iterators & filter_map instead of for-loop.
• Replace Box<dyn BufRead> with generics once comfortable with lifetimes.
• Benchmark vs system grep using `hyperfine`.

──────────────── 6. Rust vs C/C++ quick mapping ────────────────
• &str is like const char* slice; String is heap-owned like std::string.
• `?` operator is sugar for `if(res.is_err()) return Err(e)`.
• No null pointers; Option<T> forces check.
• `use std::process::exit` vs `return EXIT_FAILURE;`.

Have fun hacking—once this compiles you’ve conquered >50% of practical Rust!

Explanation of the error
------------------------
The stack-trace shows that the current thread called `Future#get()` (method `X.0i9.get(Unknown Source:47)`) while waiting for the result of a background task. Between the moment the task was scheduled and the moment `get()` was invoked, **that task had been cancelled**, so `get()` immediately threw `java.util.concurrent.CancellationException`.

Why it happens
--------------
1. **Explicit cancel()** – another thread executed `future.cancel(true /* or false */);`. On Android this often happens in an Activity’s `onStop()` or `onDestroy()`.
2. **Timeout wrapper** – `CompletableFuture.orTimeout(...)`, Guava’s `Futures.withTimeout`, or RxJava `timeout()` cancelled the task when the time limit expired.
3. **Executor shutdown** – calling `ExecutorService.shutdownNow()` implicitly cancels queued tasks.
4. A race condition or exception inside the task may trigger code that calls `cancel()` on its own Future.

Note on obfuscation
-------------------
Package prefix `X` indicates ProGuard/R8 minification. Use your `mapping.txt` to restore real class names when debugging.

How to find who calls cancel()
------------------------------
1. **Search the codebase** for `cancel(` on the same Future variable.
2. **Add a logging wrapper:**
   ```java
   future.whenComplete((v, t) -> {
       if (future.isCancelled()) {
           Log.d(TAG, "Future was cancelled", new Throwable("cancel called"));
       }
   });
   ```
3. **De-obfuscate** the stack with the mapping file so you can see real method names.
4. For Kotlin coroutines, enable `DebugProbes` to log every `Job.cancel()` with a full stack trace.

Fix / best practice
-------------------
A. **If cancellation is expected** (e.g., user navigated away):
```java
try {
    return future.get();
} catch (CancellationException e) {
    Log.w(TAG, "Background task was cancelled, using cached value", e);
    return cachedResult;
}
```

B. **If cancellation is NOT intended**
1. Remove or postpone the offending `cancel()` call.
2. Replace `shutdownNow()` with `shutdown()` + `awaitTermination()` so running tasks finish.
3. Increase timeout or move long-running I/O to a separate pool that you do not cancel aggressively.

C. **Make the task cancellation-safe**
• Inside the task, periodically check `Thread.interrupted()` and close resources.
• For CompletableFuture, propagate or ignore cancellation intentionally by catching `CancellationException`.

End-to-end example with timeout
-------------------------------
```java
ExecutorService ioPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
CompletableFuture<String> downloadFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
    try {
        return downloadBigFile();
    } catch (IOException ioe) {
        throw new CompletionException(ioe);
    }
}, ioPool).orTimeout(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

// caller thread
try {
    String data = downloadFuture.join();
    process(data);
} catch (CompletionException ce) {
    if (ce.getCause() instanceof TimeoutException) {
        // timed out; cancellation intentional
        recoverFromTimeout();
    } else {
        throw ce;
    }
}
```

Debug checklist
---------------
☐ Verify whether the task actually started (first log line inside it).
☐ Confirm if `ExecutorService` was shut down earlier than expected.
☐ Inspect Activity/Fragment lifecycle callbacks for `cancel()` calls.
☐ Enable `-Djdk.internal.httpclient.debug=true` if network timeouts are involved.

Conclusion
----------
`CancellationException` is not a JVM bug—it tells you that **your own code or a wrapper library deliberately aborted the task**. Identify who calls `cancel()`, decide if that is expected, then either handle it gracefully or stop the unwanted cancellation.

