Short answer: Yes—export your functions individually, then export a default object that contains them, or let the consumer use a namespace import.

```js
// ----- util.js (ES module) -----
// 1. Define the actual functions
export function doSomething() {
  console.log('Doing something');
}

export function doSomethingElse() {
  console.log('Doing something else');
}

// 2. Build a namespace object and make it the *default* export
const Util = {
  doSomething,
  doSomethingElse,
};
export default Util;        // There can be only ONE default
// You may keep the named exports above for tree-shaking; the object won’t stop that.
```

```js
// ----- consumer.js -----
import Util, { doSomething } from './util.js';

Util.doSomething();       // Works through the default object
Util.doSomethingElse();   // ditto

// Or, if you only need one helper, the named import is still possible and tree-shakeable:
//   doSomething();
```

Alternative without a default export:
```js
// util.js
export function doSomething() {}
export function doSomethingElse() {}

// consumer.js
import * as Util from './util.js';
Util.doSomething();
```

CommonJS variant (older Node or .cjs file):
```js
function doSomething() {}
function doSomethingElse() {}
module.exports = {
  doSomething,
  doSomethingElse,
};
// const Util = require('./util');
// Util.doSomething();
```

Key points & pitfalls:
• Only one default export is allowed per module; you cannot automatically expose multiple functions as Util without building the object yourself.
• Namespace import (`import * as Util`) is the simplest way to get “Util.someFunc” without authoring a default export at all.
• If you also export the functions by name, bundlers can tree-shake unused ones; placing everything only on the default object prevents that optimisation.
• In Node prior to ES-module support (or inside a .cjs file) you use `module.exports = { … }`, but that is CommonJS, not a true ESM default export.
• Avoid mixing CommonJS `module.exports` with `export` syntax in the same file; it throws in strict ESM context.
• Remember to set "type":"module" in package.json or use .mjs extension for ESM in Node ≥13.2.

Yes. Although many developers think of Node streams as push-based because of the ubiquitous 'data' event, Readable streams were designed to be pull-driven. The trick is to stay in paused ('readable') mode and supply data only from the _read(size) method.

Below is a fully-working example that counts from 1-N only when the consumer asks for the next chunk. The file runs unmodified with Node ≥12.

```js
// pull-number-stream.js
const { Readable } = require('stream');

class PullNumberStream extends Readable {
  constructor(max, opts = {}) {
    super({ ...opts, objectMode: true });
    this.max = max;
    this.current = 1;
  }

  // Node calls this when it wants more data (buffer < highWaterMark)
  _read(size) {
    // Simulate expensive async work
    setTimeout(() => {
      if (this.current > this.max) {
        // Signal end-of-stream
        this.push(null);
        return;
      }
      const ok = this.push(this.current++);
      // If ok === false, Node will not call _read again until
      // the consumer pulls more data.
    }, 100);
  }
}

// ───────────────────────────────
// Different consumer styles

// 1. Paused mode using .read() loop
const nums = new PullNumberStream(3);
nums.on('readable', () => {
  let chunk;
  while ((chunk = nums.read()) !== null) {
    console.log('read():', chunk);
  }
});

// 2. for-await (supported since Node 10)
(async () => {
  for await (const n of new PullNumberStream(3)) {
    console.log('for-await:', n);
  }
})();

// 3. stream/promises.pipeline (Node 15+)
const { pipeline } = require('stream/promises');
const fs = require('fs');
(async () => {
  await pipeline(
    new PullNumberStream(5),
    async function* (src) {
      for await (const n of src) yield String(n) + '\n';
    },
    fs.createWriteStream('numbers.txt')
  );
  console.log('numbers.txt written');
})();
```

Key points proven by the demo:
• Data is emitted ONLY after the consumer signals demand; there is no 'data' listener created automatically.
• _read is the sole place where push() is called, satisfying the pull contract.
• End of stream is signalled with push(null) so consumers terminate correctly.

Other pull-friendly techniques
1. `Readable.from(asyncGenerator)` – easiest for small cases:
```js
const { Readable } = require('stream');
const stream = Readable.from(async function* () {
  for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) yield i;
}(), { objectMode: true });
```
2. If you inherit data from an upstream push source, pause it when push() returns false so you don’t overflow memory.

Common pitfalls called out
• Calling push() from outside _read switches the stream to flowing mode – avoid.
• Forgetting push(null) causes for-await loops to hang.
• Mixing Buffer and objectMode without the proper flag produces runtime errors.
• Always destroy(err) or emit('error') when asynchronous work fails.

Why prefer pull?
Back-pressure is automatic, allowing you to pace expensive I/O (database reads, HTTP requests) and avoid memory bloat. You also get lazy evaluation – work is done strictly on demand.

With these patterns, Node.js provides first-class support for genuine pull-based Readable streams with no external libraries.

//==================== gaussianBlur.metal ====================
#include <metal_stdlib>
using namespace metal;

// Function constants let the CPU decide radius/sigma at run-time
constant ushort kRadius [[function_constant(0)]];   // e.g. 7  → kernel 15×15
constant float  kSigma  [[function_constant(1)]];    // e.g. 3.0

// Horizontal pass -----------------------------------------------------------
kernel void gaussianBlurHorizontal(
    texture2d<float, access::read>  inTexture  [[texture(0)]],
    texture2d<float, access::write> outTexture [[texture(1)]],
    constant float*                 kernel1D   [[buffer(0)]], // length = 2*kRadius+1
    uint2 gid [[thread_position_in_grid]])
{
    if (gid.x >= outTexture.get_width() || gid.y >= outTexture.get_height()) return;

    float4 sum = float4(0.0f);
    for (int dx = -int(kRadius); dx <= int(kRadius); ++dx) {
        uint sampleX = clamp(int(gid.x) + dx, 0, int(outTexture.get_width())  - 1);
        float  w     = kernel1D[dx + kRadius];
        sum += w * inTexture.read(uint2(sampleX, gid.y));
    }
    outTexture.write(sum, gid);
}

// Vertical pass -------------------------------------------------------------
kernel void gaussianBlurVertical(
    texture2d<float, access::read>  inTexture  [[texture(0)]], // output of horiz pass
    texture2d<float, access::write> outTexture [[texture(1)]],
    constant float*                 kernel1D   [[buffer(0)]],
    uint2 gid [[thread_position_in_grid]])
{
    if (gid.x >= outTexture.get_width() || gid.y >= outTexture.get_height()) return;

    float4 sum = float4(0.0f);
    for (int dy = -int(kRadius); dy <= int(kRadius); ++dy) {
        uint sampleY = clamp(int(gid.y) + dy, 0, int(outTexture.get_height()) - 1);
        float  w     = kernel1D[dy + kRadius];
        sum += w * inTexture.read(uint2(gid.x, sampleY));
    }
    outTexture.write(sum, gid);
}

//==================== Host-side Swift snippet ====================

// Pre-compute 1-D Gaussian kernel on CPU
func makeKernel(radius: Int, sigma: Float) -> [Float] {
    var k = [Float](repeating: 0, count: 2*radius+1)
    var sum: Float = 0
    for i in -radius...radius {
        let w = exp(-0.5 * Float(i*i) / (sigma*sigma))
        k[i+radius] = w; sum += w
    }
    // Normalise so sum == 1
    for i in 0..<k.count { k[i] /= sum }
    return k
}

let radius: UInt16 = 7
let sigma:  Float  = 3.0
let kernel1D       = makeKernel(radius: Int(radius), sigma: sigma)

// Compile compute functions with function constants
let constants = MTLFunctionConstantValues()
constants.setConstantValue(&radius,  type: .ushort, index: 0)
constants.setConstantValue(&sigma,   type: .float,  index: 1)
let horizFunc = try library.makeFunction(name: "gaussianBlurHorizontal", constantValues: constants)
let vertFunc  = try library.makeFunction(name: "gaussianBlurVertical",   constantValues: constants)
let horizPSO  = try device.makeComputePipelineState(function: horizFunc)
let vertPSO   = try device.makeComputePipelineState(function: vertFunc)

// Dispatch
let threadsPerGroup = MTLSize(width: 16, height: 16, depth: 1)
let threadgroups    = MTLSize(width: (width  + 15)/16,
                              height:(height + 15)/16,
                              depth:1)
encoder.setComputePipelineState(horizPSO)
encoder.setTexture(srcTexture, index: 0)
encoder.setTexture(tempTexture, index: 1)
encoder.setBytes(kernel1D, length: MemoryLayout<Float>.size*kernel1D.count, index: 0)
encoder.dispatchThreadgroups(threadgroups, threadsPerThreadgroup: threadsPerGroup)
// ... insert barrier / second pass with vertPSO reading tempTexture & writing dstTexture

//==================== Notes & Best Practices ====================
// • Using separable passes cuts arithmetic from O(r^2) to O(2r).
// • Edge pixels are clamped with clamp(). No out-of-bounds reads.
// • Function constants let you specialise the pipeline for each σ without rebuilding the app.
// • Use RGBA16Float or RGBA8Unorm textures; code works with any float-convertible format.
// • For very large radii (>15) consider using threadgroup shared memory to cache tiles.
// End of example

Below is a concise yet complete blueprint for building a SCIM v2 server in PHP with Slim 4. All code assumes PHP 8.1+.

1. Prerequisites & packages
   ```bash
   composer require slim/slim:^4.9 slim/psr7 nyholm/psr7
   composer require laminas/laminas-diactoros # if you prefer
   composer require league/oauth2-server doctrine/orm # persistence & auth (optional)
   ```
   Directory layout:
   ```
   /public/index.php          # Slim entry point
   /app/Controllers/          # SCIM controllers
   /app/Repositories/         # Data access
   /app/Middleware/           # Auth, content-type, logging
   /config/routes.php         # Route registration
   ```

2. Bootstrapping Slim (public/index.php)
   ```php
   use Slim\Factory\AppFactory;
   $app = AppFactory::create();
   $app->addBodyParsingMiddleware();
   $app->addRoutingMiddleware();
   $errorMiddleware = $app->addErrorMiddleware(true, true, true);
   require __DIR__ . '/../config/routes.php';
   $app->run();
   ```

3. Registering SCIM endpoints (config/routes.php)
   ```php
   use App\Controllers\UserController;
   $group = $app->group('/scim/v2', function ($group) {
       $group->get('/ServiceProviderConfig', [MetaController::class, 'serviceProvider']);
       $group->get('/ResourceTypes',          [MetaController::class, 'resourceTypes']);
       $group->get('/Schemas',                [MetaController::class, 'schemas']);

       $group->get   ('/Users',               [UserController::class, 'index']);
       $group->post  ('/Users',               [UserController::class, 'create']);
       $group->get   ('/Users/{id}',          [UserController::class, 'show']);
       $group->put   ('/Users/{id}',          [UserController::class, 'replace']);
       $group->patch ('/Users/{id}',          [UserController::class, 'update']);
       $group->delete('/Users/{id}',          [UserController::class, 'delete']);
   });
   ```
   Repeat analogously for `/Groups`.

4. Example controller (app/Controllers/UserController.php)
   ```php
   namespace App\Controllers;
   use Psr\Http\Message\ServerRequestInterface as Request;
   use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseInterface as Response;
   use App\Repositories\UserRepository;

   class UserController {
       public function index(Request $req, Response $res): Response {
           $params = $req->getQueryParams();
           $start  = (int)($params['startIndex'] ?? 1);
           $count  = (int)($params['count'] ?? 100);
           $filter = $params['filter'] ?? null;
           $users  = UserRepository::list($start, $count, $filter);
           $payload = [
               'totalResults' => $users['total'],
               'startIndex'   => $start,
               'itemsPerPage' => $count,
               'Resources'    => $users['data'],
               'schemas'      => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:ListResponse']
           ];
           $res->getBody()->write(json_encode($payload, JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR));
           return $res->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/scim+json');
       }
       // create, show, replace, update, delete similar …
   }
   ```

5. Persistence & mapping
   - Map SCIM core schema attributes (`userName`, `name.givenName`, `emails`) to DB columns.
   - Handle multivalued attributes as JSON (e.g., Doctrine ArrayType).

6. Filtering & sorting
   - Parse SCIM filter grammar (e.g., using `SoftwareHouse\Psf\ScimFilterParser` or write a lexer for `filter=emails.value sw "@example.com"`).
   - Support `sortBy`, `sortOrder` on list endpoints.

7. Patch semantics
   - Accept `application/scim+json` PATCH requests using the SCIM PatchOp schema.
   - Example: `{ "schemas" : ["urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:PatchOp"], "Operations" : [ { "op":"add", "path":"emails", "value":[{"value":"john@corp.com","type":"work"}] } ] }`.

8. Metadata endpoints
   - `/ServiceProviderConfig` → advertise auth support (`"oauth": {"supported": true}`), `patch`, `filter`, `bulk`.
   - `/ResourceTypes` and `/Schemas` → describe Users, Groups.

9. Security & validation
   - Add OAuth2 middleware (`league/oauth2-server`) to protect `/scim/*` routes; send 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer` on failure.
   - Enforce `Content-Type` and maximum payload size.
   - Escape output with `json_encode()`.

10. Error handling (RFC 7644 §3.12)
   - Use HTTP status + SCIM error JSON: `{ "schemas":["urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:Error"], "detail":"User not found", "status":"404", "scimType":"invalidValue" }`.

11. Testing & compliance
   - Add PHPUnit tests; use the official SCIM 2.0 test suite `okta/scimify` or Postman collection.

12. Deployment & scaling
   - Run under Nginx with php-fpm, enable gzip; wrap Slim in RoadRunner or Swoole for concurrency.
   - Document OpenAPI 3 spec to assist integrators.

References
- RFC 7643 (SCIM Schema), RFC 7644 (SCIM Protocol)
- https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7644
- Example open-source SCIM servers: SailPoint, FusionAuth, Simple-Scim (Node) for inspiration.

