Below is a concise, copy-paste-friendly answer that follows current Nix best practices and works on both Linux and macOS.

──────────────────────── option 1: quick one-liner ────────────────────────
# Drops you into an ad-hoc shell that has the whole toolchain
$ nix shell nixpkgs#llvmPackages_17
# or if still on nix-2.3 (legacy):
$ nix-shell -p llvmPackages_17

──────────────────────── option 2: shell.nix (legacy) ─────────────────────
with import <nixpkgs> {};
mkShell {
  buildInputs = [
    llvmPackages_17.clang    # C/C++ front-end
    llvmPackages_17.lld      # linker
    llvmPackages_17.lldb     # debugger
    llvmPackages_17.llvm     # core libs + tools (opt, llvm-ar …)
  ];
  shellHook = ''
    export CC=clang
    export CXX=clang++
  '';
}

Run:  $ nix-shell   # puts you into a reproducible env pinned to your current channel.

──────────────────────── option 3: flakes (recommended) ───────────────────
# flake.nix
{
  description = "Dev shell with LLVM 17";
  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable"; # pin exact commit if desired
  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }: let
    pkgs = import nixpkgs { system = builtins.currentSystem; };
  in {
    devShells.default = pkgs.mkShell {
      buildInputs = [
        pkgs.llvmPackages_17.clang
        pkgs.llvmPackages_17.lldb
      ];
      shellHook = ''
        export CC=clang
        export CXX=clang++
      '';
    };
  };
}

Enter with:  $ nix develop   # or nix shell .

──────────────────────── notes & pitfalls ────────────────────────────────
• llvmPackages_17 provides the *whole* suite; pluck individual components as shown to avoid packing unneeded crates.
• Don’t use `nix-env -iA llvm_17` — it is imperative, impure, and will collide with other profiles.
• On macOS you may need to run:  codesign --force --entitlements - --timestamp=none $(command -v lldb)  (see upstream issue #53672).
• If you’re on the 23.11 stable channel, llvmPackages_17 already exists; on 23.05 you must pull nixos-unstable or an overlay because that channel only has 16.
• To pin a fixed revision for reproducibility, replace the nixpkgs input with:  "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/91bf93c6c3ec".
• To use clang as default compiler in a flake-based *build* (not just devShell), set `clangStdenv` or `llvmPackages_17.stdenv`.

That’s all you need to have LLVM/Clang 17 inside a Nix shell, reproducibly and idiomatically.

Below is a concise but complete example of building a Windows service that decodes network traffic with Npcap.  The code is presented in C# .NET 6 using the SharpPcap/PacketDotNet wrappers (which ship with Npcap) because they dramatically shorten boiler-plate; the same concepts transfer to native C with the Npcap SDK.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. Prerequisites
────────────────
• Install "Npcap 1.78" (or newer) with the "Install Npcap in WinPcap API-compatible Mode" option.
• Visual Studio 2022 or "dotnet 6.0 SDK".
• NuGet packages: SharpPcap (version ≥7.0.0) and PacketDotNet.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2. Project skeleton (Service Worker template)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
> dotnet new worker -n PacketDecoderService --framework net6.0
> cd PacketDecoderService
> dotnet add package SharpPcap
> dotnet add package PacketDotNet

The Worker Service template maps 1-to-1 to a classic Windows service; Program.cs registers the service with ServiceBaseLifetime.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3. Core code (Worker.cs)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using PacketDotNet;
using SharpPcap;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Worker : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly ILogger<Worker> _logger;
    private ICaptureDevice? _device;
    public Worker(ILogger<Worker> logger) => _logger = logger;

    protected override Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        // 1) Pick the first non-loopback interface. In production, read from config.
        _device = CaptureDeviceList.Instance.First(d => !d.Description.Contains("Loopback"));
        _device.OnPacketArrival += OnPacket;
        _device.Open(DeviceMode.Promiscuous, read_timeout: 1000);
        _device.Filter = "ip and tcp";   // BPF – capture only IP/TCP traffic.
        _device.StartCapture();
        _logger.LogInformation("Capture started on {iface}", _device.Description);
        stoppingToken.Register(() => _device?.StopCapture());
        return Task.CompletedTask; // capture runs on SharpPcap’s own thread
    }

    private void OnPacket(object sender, PacketCapture e)
    {
        var p = Packet.ParsePacket(e.GetPacket().LinkLayerType, e.GetPacket().Data);
        var ip = p.Extract<IPPacket>();
        var tcp = p.Extract<TcpPacket>();
        if (ip != null && tcp != null)
        {
            _logger.LogInformation("TCP {0}:{1} -> {2}:{3} Len={4}",
                ip.SourceAddress, tcp.SourcePort, ip.DestinationAddress, tcp.DestinationPort, e.GetPacket().Data.Length);
        }
    }

    public override void Dispose()
    {
        _device?.Close();
        base.Dispose();
    }
}
```

Notes
• SharpPcap handles buffer allocation and a dedicated capture thread so the service control handler never blocks.
• Output is written to the Windows Application log by default; configure ILogger to write to file/EventLog.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4. Build & Publish
──────────────────
> dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 /p:PublishSingleFile=true

Produces `PacketDecoderService.exe` in `bin\Release\net6.0\win-x64\publish`.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5. Install the service
──────────────────────
Open an elevated PowerShell prompt and copy binaries to `C:\Program Files\PacketDecoderService`:
```powershell
sc.exe create PacketDecoderService binPath= "C:\Program Files\PacketDecoderService\PacketDecoderService.exe" start= auto obj= "NT AUTHORITY\LocalService"
sc.exe description PacketDecoderService "Captures and logs TCP/IP traffic using Npcap"
# Grant LocalService capture rights once, or install Npcap with "Support non-Admin mode".
sc.exe start PacketDecoderService
```
Run `sc query PacketDecoderService` to verify the state. Logs appear in Event Viewer → Windows Logs → Application.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
6. Graceful stop/uninstall
──────────────────────────
> sc.exe stop PacketDecoderService
> sc.exe delete PacketDecoderService

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
7. Security & Hardening
───────────────────────
• Run as LocalService, not LocalSystem.
• Restrict log file ACLs; sign the executable and enable ASLR/DEP (`/DYNAMICBASE /NXCOMPAT`).
• Use filters such as `"tcp and port 443"` to minimise sensitive payload capture.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
8. Performance considerations
────────────────────────────
• For high traffic (>1 Gbps) pin CPU affinity & use SharpPcap’s `CaptureMultiPacket` API.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
9. Native C/C++ alternative (outline)
────────────────────────────────────────
1. Call `pcap_findalldevs()` and allow the user to pick an interface.
2. Open it via `pcap_open_live()` with 65535 snaplen.
3. Start a worker thread that calls `pcap_loop(handle, -1, callback, nullptr);`.
4. In `ServiceMain` register a control handler that calls `pcap_breakloop()` on SERVICE_CONTROL_STOP.
5. Link against `wpcap.lib` and `Packet.lib`; compile with `/MD /EHsc`.

Tested on Windows 11 22H2 with Npcap 1.78, .NET 6.0.15, SharpPcap 7.0.1.

To measure a shape that sits way down in an SVG hierarchy you can lean on three built-in calls: querySelector, getBBox, and getScreenCTM.

```js
// 1. Grab the nested element (could be 10 levels deep)
const el   = document.querySelector('#mySVG g.deep g.deeper rect');

// 2. Local box (relative to the element’s own coord-system)
const box  = el.getBBox(); // {x, y, width, height}

// 3. Turn that local box into page coordinates
const svg  = el.ownerSVGElement;
const pt   = svg.createSVGPoint();

pt.x = box.x;          pt.y = box.y;
const topLeft     = pt.matrixTransform(el.getScreenCTM());
pt.x = box.x+box.width; pt.y = box.y+box.height;
const bottomRight = pt.matrixTransform(el.getScreenCTM());

const globalBBox = {
  x:      topLeft.x,
  y:      topLeft.y,
  width:  bottomRight.x - topLeft.x,
  height: bottomRight.y - topLeft.y
};
console.log(globalBBox);
```
Why this works
• getBBox() gives the rectangle BEFORE transforms.
• getScreenCTM() stacks every translate/scale/rotate from all parent <g> elements, so matrixTransform() moves the corners into the viewport.
• The same approach survives viewBox changes because CTM already contains that scale.

Need the rectangle in the element’s own space? Skip the matrix step. Need CSS-pixel space? use getBoundingClientRect() on the parent <svg> and add scrollX/scrollY. All in under 250 words!

# Container-query-like behaviour in Jetpack Compose
On the web, a *container query* lets a component decide how it looks based on the size **of its own bounding box**, not the whole viewport. In Jetpack Compose we achieve the same effect by *reading layout constraints inside a composable* and branching our UI accordingly.

## 1. The 90 % solution: BoxWithConstraints
`BoxWithConstraints` exposes its `maxWidth`/`maxHeight` in **dp** inside the composition. Because the values are stable across recompositions, switching layout inside it is cheap and safe.
```kotlin
@Composable
fun ResponsiveCard(
    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,
    breakpoint: Dp = 600.dp,          // ≈ 37.5 em on the web
    contentPadding: Dp = 16.dp,
) {
    BoxWithConstraints(modifier) {
        val isCompact = maxWidth < breakpoint

        if (isCompact) {
            // Small container → vertical arrangement
            Column(
                Modifier.fillMaxWidth().padding(contentPadding)
            ) {
                Header()
                Spacer(Modifier.height(8.dp))
                BodyText()
                PrimaryButton(Modifier.align(Alignment.End))
            }
        } else {
            // Large container → horizontal arrangement & extra detail
            Row(
                Modifier.fillMaxWidth().padding(contentPadding),
                verticalAlignment = Alignment.CenterVertically
            ) {
                Header(modifier = Modifier.weight(1f))
                Spacer(Modifier.width(24.dp))
                BodyText(modifier = Modifier.weight(2f))
                Spacer(Modifier.width(24.dp))
                Column(horizontalAlignment = Alignment.End) {
                    MetaStats()           // content that doesn’t exist in compact mode!
                    Spacer(Modifier.height(12.dp))
                    PrimaryButton()
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
*What changed?*  
• The **structure** (Column → Row)  
• The **content** (adds `MetaStats()` only when there is room).

### Usage
```kotlin
Card {
    ResponsiveCard(modifier = Modifier.fillMaxWidth())
}
```
The card will automatically redraw when its own width changes (e.g. because it is placed inside a `LazyVerticalGrid`, split-screen, or foldable device).

## 2. Fine-grained control with SubcomposeLayout
When you need to *measure* children before deciding where/if to place them (true container query), use `SubcomposeLayout`:
```kotlin
@Composable
fun QueryLayout(
    breakpoint: Dp = 400.dp,
    primary: @Composable () -> Unit,
    secondary: @Composable () -> Unit,
) {
    SubcomposeLayout { constraints ->
        // 1) Measure primary first; get width
        val pMeasurables = subcompose("primary", primary)
        val pPlaceables = pMeasurables.map { it.measure(constraints) }
        val primaryWidth = pPlaceables.maxOf { it.width }

        val showSecondary = primaryWidth.toDp() < breakpoint
        val sPlaceables = if (showSecondary) {
            subcompose("secondary", secondary).map { it.measure(constraints) }
        } else emptyList()

        val totalHeight = (pPlaceables + sPlaceables).sumOf { it.height }
        val totalWidth  = maxOf(primaryWidth, sPlaceables.maxOfOrNull { it.width } ?: 0)

        layout(totalWidth, totalHeight) {
            var y = 0
            pPlaceables.forEach { it.placeRelative(0, y); y += it.height }
            sPlaceables.forEach { it.placeRelative(0, y); y += it.height }
        }
    }
}
```
`SubcomposeLayout` keeps you in the measure/placement phase, so you avoid the *measure->recompose* loop that `Modifier.onSizeChanged` can cause.

## 3. Other building blocks & helpers
* **WindowSizeClass (Material 3)** – good when the *window* size is the deciding factor rather than the container.
* **FlowRow / FlowColumn (Accompanist or compose-foundation ≥1.6)** – automatically wrap children, similar to CSS flexbox.
* **ConstraintLayout with guidelines** – can mimic grid container queries for complex dashboards.

## 4. Caveats
1. Don’t read `IntSize` in composition outside `BoxWithConstraints`; it will cause an extra recomposition each time the size changes.
2. Always compare in **dp**, not pixels, to respect density.
3. Keep branch count small; every distinct branch increases the number of compositions Compose must keep.
4. If you need *percentage* breakpoints, convert: `val isNarrow = maxWidth < 0.5f * parentMaxWidth`.

## 5. Conclusion
While Compose has no first-class *container query* keyword, combining `BoxWithConstraints` or `SubcomposeLayout` with idiomatic branching gives you the same power with very little code.
